atthew", "targetName": "The Calling of Saint Matthew", "context": "The Calling of Saint Matthew is a quintessential example of Baroque art, framed within the broader movement of 17th-century artistic innovation."}, {"sourceSlug": "rome", "sourceName": "Rome", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "the-calling-of-saint-matthew", "targetName": "The Calling of Saint Matthew", "context": "The Calling of Saint Matthew was created and first displayed in Rome, a central hub for Baroque art during the early 1600s."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Artistic Center"}, {"name": "Contarelli Chapel, Rome", "role": "Display Location"}, {"name": "San Luigi dei Francesi, Rome", "role": "Religious Site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: The Calling of Saint Matthew", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "the-calling-of-saint-matthew"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q969377", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Calling_of_Saint_Matthew"], "tags": ["painting", "Paintings & Visual Art", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to artistic heritage and cultural identity. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Calling of Saint Matthew significantly shaped Baroque art and religious representation, influencing artists for centuries and reflecting the Catholic Church's strategies during the Counter-Reformation.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T01:04:23Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30504317321", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Painting by caravaggio Created by Caravaggio. Created c. 1609.", "newValue": "The Calling of Saint Matthew, painted by Caravaggio around 1609, stands as a monumental work of Baroque art that vividly illustrates the moment of Matthew's divine selection as an apostle. This pivotal scene occurs in a Roman tavern, where the somber atmosphere is pierced by a dramatic beam of light illuminating Matthew, surrounded by fellow tax collectors. Caravaggio's innovative use of chiaroscuro and his ability to infuse the scene with emotional intensity and realism exemplify the transition from Renaissance to Baroque art. The painting is housed in the Contarelli Chapel of San Luigi dei Francesi in Rome, a site that has become a pilgrimage for art lovers and scholars alike.

The painting captures a transformative moment within the Christian narrative, depicting the call of the tax collector Matthew, who would become one of the twelve apostles. Caravaggio's depiction emphasizes the themes of redemption and divine intervention, which resonated with the Counter-Reformation ideals of the Catholic Church as it sought to reaffirm its influence during a period of Protestant upheaval. The work was commissioned by Cardinal Matteo Contarelli, and its completion marked a significant contribution to the Baroque movement, influencing generations of artists who sought to portray spiritual narratives with dramatic realism.

